= Aegae (Macedonia)

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c9/MACEDON%2C_Aegae._Circa_510-480_BC_%28front%29.png" height="188" width="200"

Silver coin from Aegae depicting a [[ goat ]], reign of [[ Amyntas I ]] or
[[ Alexander I | alexander-i-of-macedon ]], c.510–480 BC

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Philip_II_larnax_vergina_greece.jpg/250px-Philip_II_larnax_vergina_greece.jpg" height="261" width="200"

The golden [[ larnax | larnax-archaeology ]] and the golden grave crown of
Philip II

!!Aegae!! or !!Aigai!! (Ancient Greek: Αἰγαί, //Aigaí//,
[[ Latinized | latinisation-of-names ]]: //Aegae//, Greek: Αιγές,
[[ romanized | romanization-of-greek ]]: //Aiges//) was the original capital
of [[ Macedon ]], the [[ ancient Greek | ancient-greece ]] kingdom of the
[[ Argead | argead-dynasty ]] [[ Makedones ]] in [[ Lower Macedonia ]]. Aigai
was located in the [[ Haliacmon ]] valley on the foothills of the
[[ Pierian Mountains ]], between the modern towns of [[ Vergina ]] and
Palatitsia,[[ ^1 ]][[ ^2 ]] overlooking the central
[[ Macedonian | macedonia-greece ]] plain, in northern [[ Greece ]].
[[ Alexander the Great ]] was proclaimed king of Macedon at Aigai when his
father [[ Philip II | philip-ii-of-macedon ]] was assassinated at the city's
theatre.

The city was abandoned in the 3rd century AD and was rediscovered in the
19th.[[ ^1 ]][[ ^3 ]] Three major archaeological missions have been carried
out at Aigai. The first was led by [[ Léon Heuzey ]] of the
[[ French School at Athens ]] in the 1860s; [[ Manolis Andronikos ]] led
excavations over a century later and made many important discoveries,
including the tomb of Philip II of Macedon and the [[ Golden Larnax ]] bearing
the [[ Vergina Sun ]]; and Angeliki Kottaridi led restoration efforts in the
2000s. Today it is the site of an [[ archaeological site ]] and two museums.
In 1996, the archaeological site of Aigai was inscribed on the [[ UNESCO ]]
[[ World Heritage List ]] because of its monumental significance in
[[ Western civilization ]] and exceptional architecture.[[ ^3 ]]

== Name

The name Aigai is etymologically related to the word for 'goat' (Ancient
Greek: [[ αἴξ | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/αἴξ#Ancient_Greek ]],
[[ romanized | romanization-of-ancient-greek ]]: //aíx//), and is translated
as 'goat town'.[[ ^4 ]] [[ Diodorus Siculus ]] claimed the city was so named
after [[ Perdiccas I | perdiccas-i-of-macedon ]] received an instruction by
the [[ Pythia ]] at the [[ Oracle of Delphi ]] to establish his city at a
place where "white-horned goats rest at dawn".[[ ^5 ]][[ ^6 ]] This version,
supported by [[ Thucydides ]], was the 'official' [[ founding myth ]] in the
5th century BC.[[ ^4 ]] In the
//[[ Epitome of the Philippic History | epitome-of-the-philippic-history-of-pompeius-trogus ]]//,
[[ Justin | justin-historian ]] gives a different account whereby
[[ Edessa | edessa-greece ]] was the older name of Aegae before it was
captured by [[ Caranus of Macedon ]].[[ ^7 ]] As a result, Edessa was
historically thought to have been site of Aegae.[[ ^8 ]]

Aigai also appears in the historical record as !!Aegeae/Aigeai!! (Αἰγέαι) and
!!Aegaea/Aigaia!! (Αἰγαῖα).[[ ^9 ]] The name is in the plural, a typical
feature of ancient Greek toponyms such as [[ Athens ]]
([[ Ἀθῆναι | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Ἀθῆναι#Ancient_Greek ]],
//Athenai//) and [[ Thebes | thebes-greece ]]
([[ Θῆβαι | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Θῆβαι#Ancient_Greek ]],
//Thebai//). In modern Greek the name is !!Aiges!! (Αιγές, Greek
pronunciation:
[[ [eˈʝes\] | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help%3AIPA%2FGreek ]]), showing a
typical [[ first declension | first-declension#greek-nouns ]] transition from
ancient plural ending //-ai// to the modern //-es//.

== History

In the 7th century BC, the [[ Temenids | temenus ]]' dominance led to the
Macedonians expanding and subduing local populations until the end of the 6th
century BC, and establishing the dynasty at Aigai. Ancient sources give
conflicting accounts of the origins of the
[[ Argead dynasty ]].[[ ^10 ]][[ ^11 ]]
[[ Alexander I | alexander-i-of-macedon ]] is the first truly historic figure
and, based on the line of succession, the beginnings of the Macedonian dynasty
have been traditionally dated to 750 BC.[[ ^12 ]] [[ Herodotus ]]
says[[ ^13 ]] that the [[ Argead dynasty ]] was an ancient Greek royal house
led by [[ Perdiccas I ]] who fled from [[ Argos | ancient-argos ]] in about
650 BC.[[ ^14 ]]

The Macedonians perceived the state founded by the Argeads as a typical
ancient Greek city-state (//[[ Polis ]]//), with Aegae as an urban centre
([[ ἄστυ | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ἄστυ#Ancient_Greek ]]) ruling over
the surrounding countryside
([[ χώρα | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/χώρα#Ancient_Greek ]]), not
basically different from [[ Athens | classical-athens ]] or [[ Sparta ]]; to
what degree this perceiption was historically accurate is not clear.[[ ^15 ]]
Archaeologic evidence shows that Aigai developed as an organized collection of
villages, spatially representing the aristocratic structure of tribes centred
on the power of the king. It remained so throughout its history. Indeed, Aigai
never became a large city and most of its inhabitants lived in surrounding
villages. The walled //asty// (acropolis) was built at the centre of Aigai.
From Aigai the Macedonians spread to the central part of Macedonia and
displaced the local population of [[ Pierians ]]. From 513 to 480 BC Aigai was
part of the [[ Persian Empire ]], but [[ Amyntas I ]] managed to keep its
relative independence, avoid [[ satrapy | satrap ]] and extend its
possessions. In the first half of the 5th century BC Aigai was the capital of
Macedon. Life reached unseen levels of luxury and to meet the needs of the
court merchants from all over the ancient world brought to Aigai valuable
goods including perfume, carved ornaments and jewellery.[[ ^16 ]] The city
wall was built in the 5th century, probably by [[ Perdiccas II ]]. At the end
of the 5th century [[ Archelaus I | archelaus-i-of-macedon ]] brought to his
court artists, poets, and philosophers from all over the Greek world; for
example, it was at Aigai that [[ Euripides ]] wrote and presented his last
tragedies.

At the beginning of the 4th century BC, Archelaus transferred the Macedonian
capital northeast to [[ Pella ]], which was located on a coastal waterway of
the [[ Thermaic Gulf ]], on the [[ central Macedonian | central-macedonia ]]
plain.[[ ^17 ]]
Nevertheless, Aigai retained its role as the sacred city of
the Macedonian kingdom, the site of the traditional cult centres, a royal
palace and the royal tombs. For this reason it was here that
[[ Philip II | philip-ii-of-macedon ]] was attending the wedding of his
daughter [[ Cleopatra | cleopatra-of-macedon ]] to
[[ King Alexander of Epirus | alexander-i-of-epirus ]] when he fell by the
hand of [[ Pausanias of Orestis ]][[ ^18 ]] in the theatre.[[ ^19 ]] His son
[[ Alexander the Great ]] was proclaimed king of Macedonia on the spot. The
old capital had remained the "national hearth"[[ ^20 ]] of the Macedonian
kingdom and the burial place for their kings who descended from
[[ Perdiccas | perdiccas-i-of-macedon ]]. The body of Alexander was to have
also reposed at Aigai,[[ ^21 ]] but it was taken to
[[ Memphis | memphis-egypt ]] by [[ Ptolemy I Soter ]]. The urban fabric of
the city represents a pivotal moment of transition between the classical Greek
city-state and the imperial centres of the [[ Hellenistic period ]]. The
[[ bitter struggles | wars-of-the-diadochi ]] between the
[[ heirs of Alexander | diadochi ]] in the 3rd century adversely affected the
city; in 276 BC [[ Gallic | gauls ]] mercenaries of
[[ Pyrrhus | pyrrhus-of-epirus ]] plundered many of the tombs.

After the overthrow of the Macedonian kingdom by the Romans
[[ in 168 BC | battle-of-pydna ]], both old and new capitals were destroyed,
the walls pulled down and all buildings burned. In the 1st century AD, a
landslide destroyed what had been rebuilt (excavations establish that parts
were still inhabited then). In the 2nd to 5th centuries AD the population
gradually moved from the foothills of the
[[ Pierian range | pierian-mountains ]] to the plain, and all that remained
was a small settlement whose name alone Palatitsia (palace) indicated its
former importance.

== Tomb of Philip II and other Royal Tombs of Aigai

Prior to the discoveries at [[ Vergina ]], [[ Edessa | edessa-greece ]] was
thought to be the site of Aigai. In 1977, Greek archaeologist
[[ Manolis Andronikos ]] started excavating the Great Tumulus at
Aigai[[ ^22 ]] and found that two of the four tombs in the tumulus were
undisturbed since antiquity. Moreover, these two, and particularly Tomb II,
contained fabulous treasures and objects of great quality and
sophistication.[[ ^23 ]]

Although there was much debate for some years,[[ ^24 ]][[ ^25 ]] Tomb II has
been shown to be that of Philip II as indicated by many features,[[ ^26 ]]
including the [[ greaves | greave ]], one of which was shaped consistently to
fit a leg with a misaligned tibia (Philip II was recorded as having broken his
tibia). Also, the remains of the skull show damage to the right eye caused by
the penetration of an object (historically recorded to be an arrow).[[ ^27 ]]

The most recent research gives further evidence that Tomb II contains the
remains of [[ Philip II | philip-ii-of-macedon ]].[[ ^28 ]]

