== Palace of Aigai

Palace of Aigai

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Vergina_Palace_%281990%29_-_01.jpg/250px-Vergina_Palace_%281990%29_-_01.jpg" height="144" width="220"

The palace in 1990, before the restoration

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/%CE%91%CE%BD%CE%AC%CE%BA%CF%84%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%BF_%CE%91%CE%B9%CE%B3%CF%8E%CE%BD.jpg/250px-%CE%91%CE%BD%CE%AC%CE%BA%CF%84%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%BF_%CE%91%CE%B9%CE%B3%CF%8E%CE%BD.jpg" height="165" width="220"

The palace after restoration works in the 2020s

The recently excavated palace is considered to be not only the biggest but,
together with the [[ Parthenon ]], one of the most significant buildings of
[[ classical Greece ]].[[ ^29 ]] The most important building discovered is the
monumental palace. Located on a plateau directly below the acropolis, this
building of two or perhaps three stories is centred on a large open courtyard
flanked by Doric colonnades. On the north side was a large gallery with a view
of the stage of the neighbouring theatre and the whole Macedonian plain. The
palace was sumptuously decorated, with mosaic floors, painted plastered walls,
and fine relief tiles. The masonry and architectural members were covered with
high-quality marble stucco. Excavations have dated its construction to the
reign of Philip II,[[ ^30 ]] even though he also had a palace in the capital,
[[ Pella ]]. It has been suggested that the building was designed by the
architect [[ Pytheos of Priene | pythius-of-priene ]], known for his work on
the [[ Mausoleum at Halicarnassus ]] and for his views on urban planning and
architectural proportions. The theatre, also from the second half of the 4th
century BC, was closely associated with the palace.

Nearly 30 large columns that surrounded the palace's main [[ peristyle ]] have
been reconstructed, some towering to a height of 25 ft.[[ ^31 ]] The frieze on
the peristyle's southern section has also been reconstructed.[[ ^32 ]] Over
5,000 square feet of [[ mosaics | mosaic ]] depicting a range of scenes,
including the ravishing of [[ Europa | europa-consort-of-zeus ]] and motifs
from nature have been carefully conserved.

The Palace of Aigai is the largest building of classical Greece and is the
location where [[ Alexander the Great ]] was proclaimed king in 336
BC.[[ ^33 ]]

The site of the palace lost significance for Macedonian Royalty after it (and
the rest of the city) was burned down in 168 BCE after the
[[ Battle of Pydna ]], despite the city remaining for another three
centuries.[[ ^34 ]] A landslide in the first century AD preserved what was
left of the ruins, leaving about 3–4 m of soil on top of the
monument.[[ ^34 ]]

The Palace of Aigai reopened to the public in January 2024 after an extensive
16-year restoration project.[[ ^33 ]]

